module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Basic
public import Mathlib.Order.UpperLower.Closure

/-! # Coordinate boundary bound for a monotone upper set

Source: the agent combinatorial refinement of Bierstone--Milman (1997), section 8,
Lemma 8.2 and Remarks 8.4, pp. 274--275, recorded in the DAG
(blueprint: lem-trf-monotone-boundary-box).

The exponent vectors of `MvPolynomial (Fin n) ℤ` are `Fin n →₀ ℕ`.
Their order is coordinatewise, `Finsupp.single k 1` is the coordinate vector,
and `upperClosure A` is exactly the union of the orthants generated by `A`.
The coordinate labels are zero-based; their order agrees with the paper's labels.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace MonomialDiagram

/-- A one-step boundary point has a boundary generator and bounded later coordinates.
Source: the agent refinement of Bierstone--Milman (1997), section 8, Lemma 8.2 and
Remarks 8.4, pp. 274--275 (blueprint: lem-trf-monotone-boundary-box).

Proof plan, following the four elementary steps recorded in the DAG:
1. Membership in the union of orthants means domination by a generator in `A`.
   Indeed, `x = a + b` implies `a ≤ x`, and conversely take `b t = x t - a t`.
   Thus every generator belongs to the upper set, and the set is upward closed.
2. Choose `a ∈ A` below `v + e_k`. For `t ≠ k`, this gives `a t ≤ v t`.
   If `a k ≤ v k`, then `a ≤ v`, contradicting `v ∉ upperClosure A`.
   Hence `a k = v k + 1 > 0`. Since `a k ≤ ∑ t, a t ≤ M`, also `v k < M`.
3. For `j > k`, suppose `M ≤ v j`. Transfer the whole `k` coordinate of `a`
   to `j`. The resulting vector `b` belongs to the upper set by `htransfer`.
   Its `k` coordinate is zero, its `j` coordinate is `a j + a k ≤ ∑ t, a t ≤ M ≤ v j`,
   and every other coordinate is `a t ≤ v t`. Thus `b ≤ v`, a contradiction.
   This bounds every coordinate `j ≥ k`, using the same boundary generator.
4. For `A = ∅`, membership of `v + e_k` is impossible. For the full upper set,
   nonmembership of `v` is impossible. For the last coordinate, including `n = 1`,
   Step 2 gives the entire bound and Step 3 has no indices.
-/
theorem monotone_boundary_box {n : ℕ} (hn : 1 ≤ n)
    (A : Set (Fin n →₀ ℕ)) (hA : A.Finite) (M : ℤ) (hM : 1 ≤ M)
    (hdegree : ∀ a ∈ A, (↑(∑ j : Fin n, a j) : ℤ) ≤ M)
    (htransfer : ∀ u ∈ upperClosure A, ∀ p t : Fin n, p < t →
      u - Finsupp.single p (u p) + Finsupp.single t (u p) ∈ upperClosure A)
    (k : Fin n) (v : Fin n →₀ ℕ) (hv : v ∉ upperClosure A)
    (hvk : v + Finsupp.single k 1 ∈ upperClosure A) :
    ∃ a ∈ A, a k = v k + 1 ∧ 0 < a k ∧
      (∀ t : Fin n, t ≠ k → a t ≤ v t) ∧
      (∀ j : Fin n, k ≤ j → (v j : ℤ) < M) := by
  sorry

end MonomialDiagram
